21. Conclusion
This project proposes a data-driven approach to improving logistics efficiency by combining exploratory data analysis, KPI measurement, predictive modeling, clustering, and optimization.
The first stage focuses on understanding the relational logistics database and establishing measurable performance indicators. Week 1 analysis of 85,410 trips and 170,820 delivery events produced an on-time delivery rate of 55.67%, average detention of 91.54 minutes, average fuel efficiency of 6.50 MPG, average idle time of 7.01 hours, and average trip duration of 25.01 hours. These baseline measures provide a starting point for future improvement analysis.
Python will be used throughout the analytical process for data cleaning, visualization, statistical analysis, machine learning, and optimization. Regression and classification models can later be used to predict delivery or trip performance, while clustering can identify groups of similar routes, trips, or fleet assets. Optimization techniques can then be used to translate these insights into better resource allocation and routing decisions.
The expected outcome is a structured analytical framework that helps logistics managers understand the causes of delays, improve fleet efficiency, reduce unnecessary operating costs, and increase delivery reliability.
Ultimately, the project demonstrates how data science can transform historical logistics data into actionable insights and support more efficient, cost-effective, and data-driven logistics decision-making.

